 was training coders in Rust and chasing funding. He kept saying that path did not make sense. By 2020 the swings had made the point. He stopped the altcoin work and put the time into Bitcoin art: take the ideas in this space and make them something a person can see.\nIn this episode of Share Your Bitcoin Journey, Bitcoin For The Arts founder Dion Wilson talks with him about that choice. In India he watched coins get pushed by people who could issue more of them, and he treats that power to issue as the thing that breaks the money. He says the rules eased around 2020. Buying Bitcoin, he says, is no longer treated as illegal. Selling it, he says, is taxed at 30 percent, so people buy with rupees and then leave the country to spend or sell.\nThe pictures follow that idea. For Bitcoin Racing in the UK he made the social posts and a playing card for fans. In the Bitcoin Design community he drew a Cashu note he believes Calle has been using, and a small app that grows or wilts a plant as the price moves. His first AI film treats Bitcoin as a meteorite in 2008. A gas moves through the city, some eyes turn red, and those people are infected with sound money. After that, fiat looks like a plastic skin. He was printing 21 copies of a mech suit that arrives in 2008. Another piece turns a payment into a tennis rally, a ball struck across the net about every ten minutes. Modeotec printed one of his images on a circuit board and showed it in El Salvador and in Germany. He made a mempool picture during the Knots and Core argument about data on the chain. The line on it is about not weighing the chain down.\nHe found Maya Parbhoe through a Bitcoiner Jobs post. She was building a Bitcoin school in Suriname and working toward a presidential run. He designed the banners. She had them printed and hung them on the school. A wire from the United States into India was costing 11 to 12 dollars before the work was even paid. A Lightning payment does not ask which country the file came from. Clients get a page of prices in sats.\nAsanoha asked him for work for a gallery in Denver, through Bitcoin Art Magazine. Another piece was headed to an auction on scarce.city. He was looking at a plebs conference in Goa, at El Salvador, and at Lugano. A short Bitcoin film was in progress. He tells people to catch the film screenings that run the day before a conference.\nHis note to a filmmaker who is stuck: use your voice, learn, be kind, and show proof of work. You do not have to play the politics of the old market. Be good at the work. On a swinging price, he says zoom out, learn it before you buy it, and come in with a conviction. The presses do not stop. There will only be 21 million bitcoin.\nFull video: https://youtu.be/BC-Lk4REV5I\nFIND MR. MEADOW https://www.mrmeadow.org/ X: https://x.com/meadow_mr Nostr: npub1p0wc9zluf4ptgt26gp3md80u8keg5uqn9tzwkt8v4j4e4fsa5cwqw0q4jn\nABOUT BITCOIN FOR THE ARTS 501(c)(3) supporting artists with Bitcoin grants, workshops, and education. https://www.bitcoinforthearts.org Grants: https://www.bitcoinforthearts.org/grants/apply Donate: https://www.bitcoinforthearts.org/donate\n⚡Zap 21 sats to unlock this note on\nhttps://fanfares.io/naddr/naddr1qvzqqqr6dgpzpg88x557qj6fwzsct6ryhz086ms769y0muyrsx65ptqzpf6zcck4qyvhwumn8ghj7enpdenxzun9wvhxummnw3erztnrdaksqfpcvserjdpsxqmz6ven893j6dp5xpjj6wp4v4jz6dmy8yuryefexcmxxwrrvlmxgk","created_at":1795058590,"id":"94820748c070e99151ecd812a1e5d798870fb15d2f3908017526037a8a10dab4","kind":31338,"pubkey":"a0e73529e04b4970a185e864b89e7d6e1ed148fdf08381b540ac020a742c62d5","sig":"2d2f9b683b7fe49025321d05324b283ac82b0990ec49eca80a6fe259d87844474f56fba3ae76b17d388329a5a3c15a13099d78653646eed9209d3ed37feec186","tags":[["d","8d294006-339c-440e-85ed-7d982e966c8c"],["price","21","SATS"],["title","Mr. Meadow: Bitcoin Deserves Better Visuals"],["subject","Mr. Meadow: Bitcoin Deserves Better Visuals"],["encrypted","aes-256-gcm","","https://api.fanfares.live/request-key"],["a","31990:d7d8109ee43657ce6056ada465